//RS //! D-FIELDPOL1 (card #181): computed fields — `name: T -> expr` on a struct.
//RS //! Never stored by default; every unmarked read recomputes `expr` against the
//RS //! struct's current sibling fields. `#Memo` is the one explicit stored form:
//RS //! it keeps the first answer and invalidates it when a dependency is written.
//RS //!
//RS //! This pass runs before registration (mirrors `inject_patchable_types`):
//RS //! for each struct, in order,
//RS //!   1. detect a cycle among computed fields (E0338), on the RAW parsed expr
//RS //!      (bare `Ident`s still name sibling fields at this point);
//RS //!   2. rewrite every `Ident` in a computed field's expr that names a sibling
//RS //!      field (data or computed) to `self.<field>` — the ballot's "bound
//RS //!      (read-only) to `self.<field>`" scoping, made real by substitution
//RS //!      instead of a synthetic scope, so both sema and codegen resolve it
//RS //!      through the ordinary `self.field` path (no new lowering path, I3);
//RS //!   3. synthesize a `fn <field>(self) T -> { return <rewritten expr>; }`
//RS //!      method per computed field and append it to `s.methods`, so it flows
//RS //!      through the *exact* same registration / body-checking / TIR codegen
//RS //!      pipeline as a hand-written method (S62 delegation-method precedent).
//RS //!      Codegen (`Codegen::Context`/`Codegen::TIR::lower`) marks the field
//RS //!      "computed" and routes every `Expr::Field` read of it to a call of
//RS //!      this method instead of a struct member access.
//RS //!
//RS //! The rewrite descends through statement-bearing value expressions as well as
//RS //! their final value, so dependency extraction and the synthesized getter see
//RS //! the same sibling reads. Lambda bodies keep their own lexical name scope and
//RS //! therefore resolve captures through the ordinary lambda checker.
//RS 
